        "kind": "tutorial",
        "knowledgePoint": null,
        "markdown": "The derivatives of sine, cosine, and tangent are shown in the table below:\n\n| $f(x)$ | $f'(x)$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\sin x$ | $\\cos x$ |\n| $\\cos x$ | $-\\sin x$ |\n| $\\tan x$ | $\\sec^2 x$ |\n\nNow that we know the derivatives of the standard trigonometric functions, we can differentiate any combination of them by using the properties of differentiation.\n\nFor example, let's find the derivative of $y = \\cos x + \\sin x.$\n\nHere, we can use the fact that the derivative of the sum is equal to the sum of the derivatives, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x+\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x+\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** We'll derive the derivatives shown in the table at the end of this lesson.",
        "mathAcademyId": "2618",
        "requiresCalculator": null,
